    "result_text": "The full suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`, plus its support classes) passes. The implementation is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented soft-duplicate (\"possible duplicate\") detection for owner creation. A new owner that isn't a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's `lastName` and `postcode` with a *different* telephone is now still created and flagged.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added persisted `possibleDuplicate` (Boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer) fields with getters/setters and `toString` entries.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the two read-only properties to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes them and MapStruct maps them automatically (same names/types).\n\n3. **Schema DDL** (all four dialects: hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added `possible_duplicate` and `possible_duplicate_of` columns.\n\n4. **New `PossibleDuplicateDetector`** (`rest/validation`) \u2014 a small single-responsibility component: given the candidate and the same-last-name owners, it returns the earliest-created (lowest id) existing owner sharing the candidate's postcode but with a different telephone, or empty.\n\n5. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after the existing hard-duplicate rejection (which guarantees the candidate isn't an exact duplicate), a new `flagPossibleDuplicate` helper stamps `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf`. It **reuses** the already-fetched `sameLastName` collection rather than re-querying, and reuses the existing telephone-normalization ordering so comparisons are against canonical values.\n\nThe detection runs after the identity-duplicate check and before household join, keeping each concern in its own small unit and leaving the hard/soft duplicate rules cleanly separated.",
